"Bull. Aust. Math. Soc. 100 (2019), 428–433 (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/86C7F38FE0748BFC2B857A2391185BA4/S0004972719000315a.pdf/div-class-title-on-the-regularity-of-character-degree-graphs-div.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Tong-Viet showed that his conjecture is true for k = 3 (see [5]). ... Let G be a ﬁnite group and suppose the character degree graph ∆(G) of ... By Theorem 1.2, if ∆(G) is a regular graph with odd order then ∆(G) is complete,\n[2]\nRegularity of character degree graphs\n429\nTong-Viet showed that his conjecture is true for k = 3 (see [5]). As the main result\nof this note we prove the following theorem.\nTheorem 1.2. Let G be a ﬁnite group and suppose the character degree graph ∆(G) of\nG has more than two vertices. Then ∆(G) is k-regular for some integer k if and only if\none of the following conditions holds:\n(a)\nk = 0 and G \u001b A5 × C or PSL2(8) × C for an abelian group C; or\n(b)\n∆(G) is a regular bipartite graph and in particular, if k , |ρ(G)| −1, then |ρ(G)|\nis even.\nBy Theorem 1.2, if ∆(G) is a regular graph with odd order then ∆(G) is complete,\nproviding some evidence for Tong-Viet’s conjecture.\nIf ∆(G) is a regular graph\nwith even order, then either ∆(G) is complete (as predicted by Conjecture 1.1), or\nρ(G) = X ∪Y for some disjoint nonempty sets X and Y, such that |X| = |Y| and the\nsubgraphs induced in ∆(G) by X and Y are complete.\nThroughout the paper, all groups are ﬁnite and all characters are complex characters.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Supersaturation for Oddtown and Eventown | SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/23M1586781)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 10 months ago; This partially proves a conjecture by O’Neill on the supersaturation problem of eventown.\n\n# On Supersaturation for Oddtown and Eventown\n\nAuthors: Xin Wei, Yuhao Zhao, Xiande Zhang https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4234-3610 Contact the author, and Gennian GeAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/23M1586781\n\n## Abstract.\n\nWe study the supersaturation problems of oddtown and eventown. Given a family \\(\\mathcal A\\) of subsets of an \\(n\\)-element set, let \\(op(\\mathcal A)\\) denote the number of distinct pairs \\(A,B\\in \\mathcal A\\) for which \\(|A \\cap B|\\) is odd. We show that if \\(\\mathcal A\\) consists of \\(n+s\\) odd-sized subsets, then \\(op(\\mathcal A)\\geq s+2\\), which is tight when \\(s\\le n-4\\). This disproves a conjecture by O’Neill on the supersaturation problem of oddtown. For the supersaturation problem of eventown, we show that for large enough \\(n\\), if \\(\\mathcal A\\) consists of \\(2^{\\lfloor n / 2\\rfloor }+s\\) even-sized subsets, then \\(op(\\mathcal A)\\ge s 2^{\\lfloor n / 2\\rfloor -1}\\) for any positive integer \\(s\\le 2^{\\lfloor n / 8\\rfloor } / n\\). This partially proves a conjecture by O’Neill on the supersaturation problem of eventown. Previously, the correctness of this conjecture was only verified for \\(s=1\\) and 2. We further provide a lower bound which is a factor of two away from the conjectured bound for eventown, that is, \\(op(\\mathcal{A})\\ge s 2^{\\lfloor n / 2\\rfloor -2}\\) for general \\(n\\) and \\(s\\) by using discrete Fourier analysis. Finally, some asymptotic results for the lower bounds of \\(op(\\mathcal A)\\) are given when \\(s\\) is large for both problems.\n\n### Keywords\n\n  1. oddtown\n  2. eventown\n  3. supersaturation\n  4. intersecting set families\n\n### MSC codes\n\n  1. 05D05\n\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n19.\n\nH. Kurzweil and B. Stellmacher, The Theory of Finite Groups: An Introduction, Vol. 1, Springer, 2004.\n\nCrossref\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n20.\n\nJ. O’Neill, A short note on supersaturation for oddtown and eventown, Discrete Appl. Math., 334 (2023), pp. 63–67, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2023.03.003.\n\nWeb of Science\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n21.\n\nJ. O’Neill and J. Verstraëte, A note on \\(k\\)-wise oddtown problems, Graphs Combin., 38 (2022), pp. 1–12, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00373-022-02504-z.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n22.\n\nH. S. Snevily, A generalization of the Ray-Chaudhuri-Wilson theorem, J. Combin. Des., 3 (1995), pp. 349–352, https://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.3180030505.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n23.\n\nE. Sperner, Ein Satz über Untermengen einer endlichen Menge, Math. Z., 27 (1928), pp. 544–548, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01171114.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n24.\n\nE. M. Stein and R. Shakarchi, Fourier Analysis: An Introduction, Vol. 1, Princeton University Press, 2011.\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n25.\n\nB. Sudakov and P. Vieira, Two remarks on eventown and oddtown problems, SIAM J. Discrete Math., 32 (2018), pp. 280–295, https://doi.org/10.1137/16M1100666.\n\nAbstract\n\nWeb of Science\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupersaturation for Eventown via Generator Switching (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16321)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Title: Supersaturation for Eventown via Generator Switching ... O'Neill conjectured that if $|\\mathcal F|=2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}+s$, then $e(\\mathcal F)\\ge s\\,2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1}$ for \\[ 1\\le s\\le 2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}-2^{\\lfloor n/4\\rfloor}. \\] Previously, the conjecture was known for $s=1,2$, and, for $s\\le 2^{\\lfloor n/8\\rfloor}/n$ with $n$ sufficiently large.\nTitle: Supersaturation for Eventown via Generator Switching\nAuthors: Zicheng Han, Xiande Zhang, Yuhao Zhao\nDate: Mon Aug 17 09:26:26 2026\n\nAn eventown family is a family of even-sized subsets of $[n]$ in which every two distinct members have an even-sized intersection. A classical theorem of Berlekamp and Graver shows that the maximum size of such a family is $2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$. The supersaturation problem for eventown asks how many odd-intersection pairs must occur when this extremal bound is exceeded. For a family $\\mathcal F$ of even-sized subsets of $[n]$, let $e(\\mathcal F)$ denote the number of unordered pairs whose intersection size is odd. O'Neill conjectured that if $|\\mathcal F|=2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}+s$, then $e(\\mathcal F)\\ge s\\,2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1}$ for \\[ 1\\le s\\le 2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}-2^{\\lfloor n/4\\rfloor}. \\] Previously, the conjecture was known for $s=1,2$, and, for $s\\le 2^{\\lfloor n/8\\rfloor}/n$ with $n$ sufficiently large. We prove the conjectured bound for \\[ 1\\le s\\le \\frac{2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}}{26}, \\] extending the known range to a fixed positive proportion of the extremal eventown size. The bound is sharp throughout this range. As further consequences, we derive a lower bound valid for arbitrary excess $s$, which improves the previously known estimate in an additional range. We also establish stability and removal results for families of extremal size satisfying $e(\\mathcal F)<2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1}$, showing that such a family is close to an extremal eventown family and can be made eventown by deleting a small number of its members.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRegular character-graphs whose eigenvalues are greater than or equal to -2 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05837)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Tong-viet posed the conjecture that if $\\Delta(G)$ is $k$-regular for some integer $k\\geqslant 2$, then $\\Delta(G)$ is either a complete graph or a cocktail party graph. In this paper, we show that his conjecture is true for all regular character-graphs whose eigenvalues are in the interval $[-2, \\infty )$.\nTitle: Regular character-graphs whose eigenvalues are greater than or equal to -2\nAuthors: Mahdi Ebrahimi, Maryam Khatami, Zohreh Mirzaei\nDate: Tue Jul 13 04:10:19 2021\n\nLet $G$ be a finite group and $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ be the set of all complex irreducible characters of $G$. The character-graph $\\Delta(G)$ associated to $G$, is a graph whose vertex set is the set of primes which divide the degrees of some characters in $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ and two distinct primes $p$ and $q$ are adjacent in $\\Delta(G)$ if the product $pq$ divides $\\chi(1)$, for some $\\chi\\in\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$. Tong-viet posed the conjecture that if $\\Delta(G)$ is $k$-regular for some integer $k\\geqslant 2$, then $\\Delta(G)$ is either a complete graph or a cocktail party graph. In this paper, we show that his conjecture is true for all regular character-graphs whose eigenvalues are in the interval $[-2, \\infty )$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn supersaturation for oddtown and eventown (https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05586)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; This partially proves a conjecture by O'Neill on the supersaturation problem of eventown.\nTitle: On supersaturation for oddtown and eventown\nAuthors: Xin Wei, Yuhao Zhao, Xiande Zhang, Gennian Ge\nDate: Sat Feb 11 03:24:31 2023\n\nWe study the supersaturation problems of oddtown and eventown. Given a family $\\mathcal A$ of subsets of an $n$ element set, let $op(\\mathcal A)$ denote the number of distinct pairs $A,B\\in \\mathcal A$ for which $|A \\cap B|$ is odd. We show that if $\\mathcal A$ consists of $n+s$ odd-sized subsets, then $op(\\mathcal A)\\geq s+2$, which is tight when $s\\le n-4$. This disproves a conjecture by O'Neill on the supersaturation problem of oddtown. For the supersaturation problem of eventown, we show that for large enough $n$, if $\\mathcal A$ consists of $2^{\\lfloor \\frac n 2\\rfloor}+s$ even-sized subsets, then $op(\\mathcal A)\\ge s\\cdot2^{\\lfloor \\frac n 2\\rfloor-1} $ for any positive integer $s\\le \\frac{2^{\\lfloor\\frac n 8\\rfloor}} n$. This partially proves a conjecture by O'Neill on the supersaturation problem of eventown. Previously, the correctness of this conjecture was only verified for $s=1$ and $2$. We further provide a twice weaker lower bound in this conjecture for eventown, that is $op(\\mathcal{A})\\ge s\\cdot 2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-2}$ for general $n$ and $s$ by using discrete Fourier analysis. Finally, some asymptotic results for the lower bounds of $op(\\mathcal A)$ are given when $s$ is large for both problems.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstructions for supersaturation of eventown problems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.20812)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Title: Constructions for supersaturation of eventown problems ... This extends the range of $s$ in a conjecture proposed by O'Neill from $2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\rfloor}-2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{4} \\rfloor}$ to $2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\rfloor}-2$.\nTitle: Constructions for supersaturation of eventown problems\nAuthors: Xiaolei Niu, Yinghui Hang, Haitao Cao\nDate: Thu Jul 23 00:55:47 2026\n\nIn this paper, we study the supersaturation problems of eventown. Given a family $\\mathcal{A}$ of subsets of an $n$ element set, let op$(\\mathcal{A})$ denote the number of distinct pairs $A,B\\in \\mathcal{A}$ for which $|A\\cap B|$ is odd. We give extremal eventown constructions and show that for fixed $s\\le2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\rfloor}-2$, there exists a collection of $2^{\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor}+s$ even-sized subsets of an $n$ element set that contains exactly $s\\cdot 2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\rfloor-1}$ pairwise intersections of odd size. This extends the range of $s$ in a conjecture proposed by O'Neill from $2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\rfloor}-2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{4} \\rfloor}$ to $2^{\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\rfloor}-2$. We also give a construction using symmetric designs to prove that when $k$ is even and $4k-1$ is a prime power, there exists a collection of $2^{\\lfloor\\frac{4k-1}{2}\\rfloor}+s$ even-sized subsets of a $4k-1$ element set $\\mathcal{A}_s$ with $op(\\mathcal{A}_s)=s \\cdot 2^{{\\lfloor\\frac{4k-1}{2}\\rfloor}-1}$, $1\\leq s\\leq4k-1$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA SAN DIEGOCombinatorics of intersecting set systemsA dissertation submitted in partial satisfaction of therequirements for the degreeDoctor of PhilosophyMathematicsJason O’NeillCommittee in charge:Professor Jacques Verstraëte, Chair (https://escholarship.org/content/qt87t0v7rk/qt87t0v7rk_noSplash_13f9643c5fc0810e0fe0a6b81617cf62.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; In Chapter 3, we explore the *supersaturation* versions of the oddtown and eventown\nwhich satisfies combinatorial constraints.  In this setting, we necessarily have at least\none instance of the forbidden combinatorial constraint if we have one more than the\nmaximum size. Supersaturation problems entail the minimum number of instances of the\nforbidden combinatorial constraint if we have more than the maximum size. The notion\nof supersaturation stems from the fact that, in certain instances, given one more than the\nextremal amount, we are guaranteed many instances of the forbidden property. In other\nwords, the forbidden property saturates as we get slightly more than the maximum.The first instance of supersaturation in extremal combinatorics comes from triangle-\nfree graphs. Mantel [Man07] proved that any $n$-vertex triangle-free graph has at most\n$\\lfloor n^2/4\\rfloor$ edges. This result is easily seen to be best possible by taking a complete bipartite\ngraph with part sizes nearly equal. The Erdős–Rademacher problem in extremal combi-\nnatorics involves the minimum number of triangles in an $n$-vertex graph with $\\lfloor n^2/4\\rfloor + s$\nedges. Erdős [Erd62] proved that there are at least $s\\cdot\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$ triangles in an $n$-vertex graph\nwith $\\lfloor n^2/4\\rfloor + s$ edges for $s\\le 3$.In Chapter 3, we explore the *supersaturation* versions of the oddtown and eventown\n\nproblems: given slightly more than \\(n\\) odd-sized subsets (resp. \\(2^{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\\) even-sized subsets)\n\nof an \\(n\\) element set, how many pairs of sets from our collection must have an odd number\n\nof elements in common?For a collection, or set family \\(\\mathcal{A}\\), let \\(\\operatorname{op}(\\mathcal{A})\\) denote the number of distinct pairs\nof sets \\(A,B\\in\\mathcal{A}\\) for which \\(|A\\cap B|\\) is odd. For convenience, suppose \\(n=2k=4l\\)\nand let \\(X_1,\\dots,X_l\\) be pairwise disjoint sets with \\(X_i=\\{x_{1,i},x_{2,i},x_{3,i},x_{4,i}\\}\\). Given \\(X_i\\), let13--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\narXiv:2302.05586v2  [math.CO]  15 Jul 2023 (https://scispace.com/pdf/on-supersaturation-for-oddtown-and-eventown-1qn7jy06.pdf)\nciteturn1search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; This partially proves a conjecture by O’Neill on the supersaturation problem of eventown.\nOn supersaturation for oddtown and eventown\n\nXin Wei, Yuhao Zhao, Xiande Zhang and Gennian Ge\n\nAbstract\n\nWe study the supersaturation problems of oddtown and eventown. Given a family \\(\\mathcal{A}\\) of subsets of an \\(n\\) element set, let \\(op(\\mathcal{A})\\) denote the number of distinct pairs \\(A, B \\in \\mathcal{A}\\) for which \\(|A \\cap B|\\) is odd. We show that if \\(\\mathcal{A}\\) consists of \\(n + s\\) odd-sized subsets, then \\(op(\\mathcal{A}) \\ge s + 2\\), which is tight when \\(s \\le n - 4\\). This disproves a conjecture by O’Neill on the supersaturation problem of oddtown. For the supersaturation problem of eventown, we show that for large enough \\(n\\), if \\(\\mathcal{A}\\) consists of \\(2^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor} + s\\) even-sized subsets, then \\(op(\\mathcal{A}) \\ge s \\cdot 2^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor - 1}\\) for any positive integer \\(s \\le 2^{\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor}/n\\). This partially proves a conjecture by O’Neill on the supersaturation problem of eventown. Previously, the correctness of this conjecture was only verified for \\(s = 1\\) and \\(2\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeekly Seminars | Department of Mathematics (https://mathematics.ucsd.edu/news-events/seminars/weekly-full/2021/40)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ##### Jason O'Neill ... Towards supersaturation for oddtown and eventown\n\n#### Tuesday, October 5, 2021 - 11:00 AM\n\n##### \n\nZoom, email djekel@ucsd.edu for meeting details\n\n##### Department of Mathematics,\nUniversity of California San Diego\n\n****************************\n\n#### ABACUS\n\n##### Jason O'Neill\n\n##### University of California, San Diego\n\n### \n\nTowards supersaturation for oddtown and eventown\n\n##### Abstract:\n\nIn an $n$-resident town, Oddtown, all of their clubs must satisfy the following properties: all clubs must have an odd number of members and amongst any two distinct clubs, there must be an even number of residents in common. The classical oddtown theorem states that any such town can have at most $n$ clubs. In this talk, we explore how the residents can have $n+1$ clubs of odd size and minimize the chance of the town catching them. That is, we'd like to minimize the number of pairs of clubs with an odd number of members in common. We will also explore a similar problem with Eventown.\n\n#### Tuesday, October 5, 2021 - 12:30 PM\n\n##### \n\nZoom -- email jmoneill@ucsd.edu for link\n\n##### Department of Mathematics,\nUniversity of California San Diego\n\n****************************\n\n#### Zoom for Thought\n\n##### Thomas Grubb\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Difference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354088064_Difference_sets_and_tri-weight_linear_codes_from_trinomials_over_binary_fields)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In the course of confirming the conjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every element of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preimages. ... We also give a partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that linear codes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# Difference sets and tri-weight linear codes from trinomials over binary fields\n\n  * August 2021\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2108.10042\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nOmran Ahmadi Darvishvand\n\n  * Institute for Research in Fundamental Sciences\n\nMasoud Shafaeiabr\n\nMasoud Shafaeiabr\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe confirm a conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that the punctured value-sets of a list of eleven trinomials over odd-degree extensions of the binary field give rise to difference sets with Singer parameters. In the course of confirming the conjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every element of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preimages. We also give a partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding~\\cite{Ding-Discrete} claiming that linear codes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:2108.10042v1 [math.CO] 23 Aug 2021\n\nDIFFERENCE SETS AND TRI-WEIGHT LINEAR CODES FROM\n\nTRINOMIALS OVER BINARY FIELDS\n\nOMRAN AHMADI AND MASOUD SHAFAEIABR\n\nAbstract. We conﬁrm a conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding [5] claiming that the punctured\n\nvalue-sets of a list of eleven trinomials over odd-degree extensions of the binary ﬁeld\n\ngive rise to diﬀerence sets with Singer parameters. In the course of conﬁrming the\n\nconjecture, we show that these trinomials share the remarkable property that every\n\nelement of the value-set of each trinomial has either one or four preiamges . We also\n\ngive partial resolution of another conjecture of Cun Sheng Ding [5] claiming that linear\n\ncodes constructed from those eleven trinomials are tri-weight.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nLet G be a cyclic group of order v. A subset D of size k of G is a cyclic diﬀerence\n\nset with parameter (v, k, λ)if for every non-identity element g of G the number of (x, y)\n\nsolutions of the equation g=xy−1 with x, y ∈ D is exactly λ.\n\nDiﬀerence sets have many applications in the construction of combinatorial designs,\n\nerror correcting codes, sequences with good correlation properties as well as the study of\n\nﬁnite geometries and cryptographic functions.\n\nAs it is the case with the construction of many other combinatorial objects, the theory\n\nof ﬁnite ﬁelds is of great importance in the construction of diﬀerence sets. Cyclic diﬀerence\n\nsets of special interest which are constructed from ﬁnite ﬁelds are the so called diﬀerence\n\nsets with Singer parameters for which either\n\nv=q n−1\n\nq−1, k =q n−1−1\n\nq−1, λ =q n−2−1\n\nq−1\n\nor\n\nv=q n−1\n\nq−1, k =q n−1, λ =q n−2(q−1)\n\nwhere q is a prime power and n≥3.\n\nIn this paper, we are interested in the construction of cyclic diﬀerence sets and linear\n\ncodes from polynomials and rational functions deﬁned over the binary ﬁnite ﬁeld F 2 m with\n\n2 m elements where m is an odd number. Our main result is the following theorem stated\n\nas Conjecture 36 in [5].\n\nTheorem 1. Let m≥5 be an odd number, F 2 m denote the degree-m extension of the\n\nbinary ﬁeld F 2, and F 2 m[x]denote the univar